--TEST--
`Core\Hash::hmac` answers RFC 4231's vectors for a key shorter than the block, a binary key and a key longer than the block, and `Core\Hash::equals` answers over operands of unequal length — spec § 11, `rule:types/bytes` and `rule:types/conversion`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// RFC 2104's construction pads the key to the hash's block size, or hashes it
// first when it is longer than one — so the key's *length relative to the
// block* is the axis this member can get wrong, and it is the axis RFC 4231's
// suite is built around. `hash-computes-every-digest-and-authenticates-with-hmac.nvst`
// writes only Case 2, whose key is four octets.

// RFC 4231 Case 1: a 20-octet key, shorter than either block size, over eight
// octets of text. All three `StrongDigest` cases, since the block size is 64
// for SHA-256 and 128 for the other two and the padding differs with it.
bytes $key20 = Core\Encoding::fromHex(Core\Str::repeat("0b", 20));
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $key20, Core\Digest::Sha256)), "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $key20, Core\Digest::Sha384)), "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $key20, Core\Digest::Sha512)), "\n";

// RFC 4231 Case 4: a 25-octet key of ascending octets and 50 octets of `cd`.
// Neither is text — `secret bytes $key` is a `bytes` parameter and the data
// side takes `bytes|string`, so a key that came out of a key-derivation and a
// message that came off a socket both arrive without a UTF-8 detour (`rule:types/bytes` and `rule:types/conversion`
// ).
bytes $key25 = Core\Encoding::fromHex("0102030405060708090a0b0c0d0e0f10111213141516171819");
bytes $cd50 = Core\Encoding::fromHex(Core\Str::repeat("cd", 50));
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac($cd50, $key25, Core\Digest::Sha256)), "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac($cd50, $key25, Core\Digest::Sha512)), "\n";

// RFC 4231 Case 6: a 131-octet key, longer than both block sizes, so the
// construction hashes it down before padding it.
bytes $key131 = Core\Encoding::fromHex(Core\Str::repeat("aa", 131));
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac(
    "Test Using Larger Than Block-Size Key - Hash Key First", $key131, Core\Digest::Sha256
)), "\n";
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac(
    "Test Using Larger Than Block-Size Key - Hash Key First", $key131, Core\Digest::Sha384
)), "\n";

// RFC 4231 Case 7: the same over-long key with data longer than a block too,
// which is the row where a mistake in the key path and a mistake in the data
// path would otherwise be able to hide behind each other.
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Hash::hmac(
    "This is a test using a larger than block-size key and a larger than block-size data. The key needs to be hashed before being used by the HMAC algorithm.",
    $key131,
    Core\Digest::Sha512
)), "\n";

// The two key rules RFC 2104 states, asserted as identities rather than as
// pasted constants — an identity that holds is a stronger claim than a hex
// string, because it stays true if the algorithm behind the case changes.
//
// A key longer than the block is exactly `Core\Hash::of` of that key, used as
// the key. The digest that reduces it is the HMAC's own, so the SHA-256 row
// reduces with SHA-256 and the SHA-512 row with SHA-512.
if (Core\Hash::equals(
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $key131, Core\Digest::Sha256),
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", Core\Hash::of($key131, Core\Digest::Sha256), Core\Digest::Sha256)
)) {
    echo "an over-long key is hashed first\n";
}
if (Core\Hash::equals(
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $key131, Core\Digest::Sha512),
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", Core\Hash::of($key131, Core\Digest::Sha512), Core\Digest::Sha512)
)) {
    echo "and reduced with the HMAC's own digest\n";
}

// A key shorter than the block is zero-padded up to it, so the empty key —
// which RFC 4231 does not publish and which a caller reaches by mistake rather
// than on purpose — is the all-zero block. It is a real key to this member, not
// an error: nothing in the construction is undefined for it, and refusing it
// here would be a rule a program could not discover from § 11's signature.
bytes $noKey = Core\Encoding::fromHex("");
if (Core\Hash::equals(
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", $noKey, Core\Digest::Sha256),
    Core\Hash::hmac("Hi There", Core\Encoding::fromHex(Core\Str::repeat("00", 64)), Core\Digest::Sha256)
)) {
    echo "an empty key is a zero block\n";
}

// § 11's `equals` is constant-time in *where* two equal-length operands
// diverge and says nothing about their lengths — a digest's width is a
// property of its algorithm and never a secret — so a length mismatch is
// answered immediately and answered `false`. These are the rows that pin the
// answer rather than the timing: a prefix is not a match, and two operands of
// different algorithms are not either.
if (!Core\Hash::equals(Core\Encoding::fromHex("00ff"), Core\Encoding::fromHex("00ff00"))) {
    echo "a prefix is not equal\n";
}
if (!Core\Hash::equals(
    Core\Hash::of("abc", Core\Digest::Sha256),
    Core\Hash::of("abc", Core\Digest::Sha384)
)) {
    echo "nor is the same input under two algorithms\n";
}
if (Core\Hash::equals($noKey, Core\Encoding::fromHex(""))) {
    echo "two empty operands are equal\n";
}
if (!Core\Hash::equals($noKey, Core\Encoding::fromHex("00"))) {
    echo "and empty is not one zero octet\n";
}
--EXPECT--
